From: Bo-Cun Chen <bc-bocun.chen@mediatek.com>
Subject: net: ethernet: mtk_wed: add WDMA disable flow to WiFi L1 SER

Backport of MTK SDK patch
"[PATCH 13/15] net: ethernet: mtk_wed: add WDMA disable flow to WiFi L1
SER" (999-wed-13, recover the original via
`git show eef2a51256:target/linux/mediatek/patches-6.12/...`).

Original rationale (SDK commit message): without this, the WED driver
cannot prevent packets from entering WDMA during WiFi L1 SER, causing
incomplete packets to get stuck in the PSE, and the PSE buffer
management transitions to a chaotic state.

Motivation on this box: the 2026-07-10 SER quiesce test ran with live
clients pushing traffic; the mt7915 MCU never answered again after the
reset (FW_LOG_2_HOST 0x13ed timeout loop). This closes the window where
the PPE keeps feeding WDMA while WED/WPDMA are being torn down.

Adaptations vs the SDK original:
- SDK's PSE_WDMA_PORT(id)/mtk_pse_wdma_enable assumed the v2+ port map
  (8/9/13, second FE_GLO_CFG instance). NETSYSv1 maps WDMA to PSE port
  3 (see the !mtk_is_netsys_v2_or_greater() branch in
  mtk_flow_set_output_device()), single FE_GLO_CFG at 0x00, link-down
  bit BIT(11) — the same register+bit family mtk_prepare_for_reset()
  already uses. Helper is therefore v1-only and takes no port id.
- Call sites gated with mtk_wed_is_v1() so v2/v3 behavior is unchanged
  (this tree only drives MT7622, but keep it explicit).

mtk_wed_reset_dma() is only reachable as the .reset_dma op (SER path);
mtk_wed_start() always follows, so the disable/enable pairing is
balanced. Both WED instances share PSE port 3 on v1; the bit toggle is
idempotent.

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.h
@@ -1636,6 +1636,25 @@
 u32 mtk_r32(struct mtk_eth *eth, unsigned reg);
 u32 mtk_m32(struct mtk_eth *eth, u32 mask, u32 set, unsigned int reg);
 
+/* Stop/resume traffic entering WDMA by toggling the FE link-down bit
+ * of the PSE WDMA port, so no partial packets lodge in the PSE while
+ * WED is reset during WiFi L1 SER. NETSYSv1 only: v1 maps WDMA to PSE
+ * port 3 (see mtk_flow_set_output_device()); v2/v3 WDMA ports (8/9/13)
+ * may need the second FE_GLO_CFG instance and are not wired here.
+ */
+static inline void mtk_pse_wdma_enable(struct mtk_eth *eth, bool enable)
+{
+	u32 port = 3;	/* NETSYSv1 PSE WDMA port */
+	u32 val;
+
+	val = mtk_r32(eth, MTK_FE_GLO_CFG(port));
+	if (enable)
+		val &= ~MTK_FE_LINK_DOWN_P(port);
+	else
+		val |= MTK_FE_LINK_DOWN_P(port);
+	mtk_w32(eth, val, MTK_FE_GLO_CFG(port));
+}
+
 int mtk_gmac_sgmii_path_setup(struct mtk_eth *eth, int mac_id);
 int mtk_gmac_2p5gphy_path_setup(struct mtk_eth *eth, int mac_id);
 int mtk_gmac_gephy_path_setup(struct mtk_eth *eth, int mac_id);
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_wed.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_wed.c
@@ -1888,6 +1888,9 @@
 	u32 val;
 	int i;
 
+	if (mtk_wed_is_v1(dev->hw))
+		mtk_pse_wdma_enable(dev->hw->eth, false);
+
 	wed_bc_mark(WED_BC_PH_RESET_DMA, 30, 0);
 
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(dev->tx_ring); i++) {
@@ -2553,6 +2556,8 @@
 	mtk_wed_amsdu_init(dev);
 
 	mtk_wed_dma_enable(dev);
+	if (mtk_wed_is_v1(dev->hw))
+		mtk_pse_wdma_enable(dev->hw->eth, true);
 	dev->running = true;
 }
 
